## Context

Quarto exits 0 when a `{{< meta key >}}` macro names a key that is absent from
the generated vars file. It silently writes the literal string `?meta:key` into
the rendered PDF. Nothing in the suite can see this: the render succeeds, the
tests pass, and the defect is visible only to a human reading the finished
document.

Found twice in the 0327/0329 raid (2026-07-27), by two independent verification
gates. Ticket 0329 alone moved 15 facts behind macros; the data paper now uses
77 macro keys in total. Every number a referee will check is behind this
mechanism, and its only gate today is someone remembering to look.

The nearest existing guard, `tests/test_doc_vars_completeness.py`, cross-checks
prose usage against `DOC_VARS` — a Python dict in `compute_vars.py`. That is a
source-to-source check, one layer removed from the generated
`data-paper-vars.yml` that Quarto actually reads. It never invokes Quarto and
never inspects rendered output. A grep of the whole suite finds no test that
runs `quarto render` and looks for `?meta:`.

All 77 keys resolve correctly today — verified by hand during the raid. That is
incidental, not gated, and the next added macro is unguarded again.

This is the same defect class as ticket 0325 (a byte-correct source file that
published an inverted filter recipe because nothing tested the *rendered*
output). The lesson recorded there was "test the rendered page, not the emitted
source"; the data paper's numbers are still on the wrong side of it.

## Relevant files

- `tests/test_doc_vars_completeness.py` — the existing source-to-source guard
- `scripts/analysis/compute_vars.py` — `DOC_VARS`, and the vars-file emitters
- `deliverables/data-paper/data-paper.qmd` — 77 macro uses
- `deliverables/data-paper/data-paper.mk` — the render rule

## Actions

1. Add a render-level guard that fails when a rendered deliverable contains the
   literal `?meta:`. Extracting text from the built PDF (`pdftotext`) is the
   honest oracle; a Quarto render into a temp dir is the cheaper one.
2. Decide the tier deliberately: a real render needs the Quarto toolchain and
   the vars artifact, so it belongs in `integration` or `slow`, not the fast
   loop. It must run under `make check`.
3. If a full render is too heavy for routine use, add the cheap complement as
   well — resolve every `{{< meta X >}}` in the qmd and its includes against the
   generated vars YAML directly, rather than against the `DOC_VARS` dict. This
   closes the one-layer-removed gap even when no render happens.

## Test

- Red first: declare a macro naming a key that does not exist, render, and
  assert the guard fails. The guard must fail on that input before it is
  trusted on real input.
- Assert the guard passes on the current deliverables (77 keys, all resolving).

## Verification

- [ ] A deliberately undeclared macro key makes the suite fail
- [ ] The guard reads rendered output, or the generated vars file, not `DOC_VARS`
- [ ] Runs under `make check`; correctly tiered

## Invariants

- No number in the data paper reaches the reader as `?meta:`.
- The guard must be able to fail. A check that cannot go red certifies nothing
  (see 0327, where the ledger's own closure guard was algebraically dependent
  and would have passed a ledger 9,835 works too high).

## Exit criteria

- [ ] An undeclared `{{< meta >}}` key cannot reach a rendered deliverable
      without a test failing
